From 1e42ba471e706bbe90eb887a1aed0f26d8fcf015 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kartheek Palla <palla.kartheekreddy@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 19:17:40 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] feat(oid4vc-service): issue through a Sunbird RC Authority
 Service and carry the linkage
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds an `authority` claim source. It asks a Sunbird RC Authority Service to issue, server to
server, and carries the resulting credential identifier into the SD-JWT as
`authorityCredentialId`.

This makes two credentials for one subject, deliberately, and they are not the same
credential. The Authority Service's JSON-LD credential is the authoritative status and
provenance anchor, and never leaves the Authority. The SD-JWT is the holder-bound
presentation credential the wallet holds and a verifier sees. Neither replaces the other:
one is what a status answer is about, the other is what is presented.

The identifier can only come from an Authority response, and that is structural rather than
checked. This provider is the only thing in the issuance path that talks to the Authority
Service, `resolve` is called with a subject read from a validated token, and nothing a
wallet, holder or caller sends reaches it. A caller-supplied value is not rejected; there is
no path by which it could arrive. There is a test that passes a hostile
`authorityCredentialId` in the request and finds the issued one in the claims.

Fits the existing ClaimSourceProvider seam rather than adding plumbing, so the token, offer
and signing paths are untouched and holder binding is unchanged. Selected per credential type
through CLAIM_SOURCE_MAP, as `registry` and named HTTP sources already are.

Issuance is idempotent on what is being issued rather than on when it was asked. The key is a
hash of profile, record and subject, so a wallet retrying after a dropped response gets the
same Authority credential instead of minting a second anchor for one presentation credential —
which would break the one-to-one linkage the status answer depends on. The subject is hashed
into the key rather than sent as itself, so an identifier from a validated token is not
echoed into another service's logs.

Claims run through the same resolver every other source uses, so only attributes the
credential type DECLARES survive. An Authority returning more than was asked for cannot widen
the credential any more than an issuer's own endpoint can — tested with a response carrying a
national identifier and a district, neither of which reaches the claims.

Failure is closed throughout. Unreachable, timed out, refused, a profile with no binding, and
an issuance that returns no identifier are all refusals; a credential that cannot be
status-checked is not minted. A subject with no record stays distinguishable from a source
that is down, because the two send an operator to different places. An ambiguous record match
is refused rather than resolved to the first, since issuing from it would attach a credential
to whichever record happened to sort first with nothing downstream able to tell.

The holder's token is deliberately not forwarded. This call is made as the issuing service,
and a holder must not be able to act as it.

167 tests, 16 suites, 0 failures — the existing 154 unchanged plus 13 here.
